00:01:01.111 --> 00:01:02.131
Hell Creek's up there.

00:01:02.191 --> 00:01:04.792
I don't know how you'd push this one out of the top five.

00:01:04.952 --> 00:01:09.614
The Burgess Shale is a fossil locality in British Columbia up in Canada

00:01:09.965 --> 00:01:16.008
that hosts an absolutely phenomenal Cambrian ecosystem.

00:01:16.468 --> 00:01:20.910
Famous not only for being in the Cambrian, and thus related to the Cambrian explosion,

00:01:21.430 --> 00:01:25.793
but also for incredible preservation of soft tissues.

00:01:26.373 --> 00:01:29.254
Like, most of the fossils are soft body fossils.

00:01:29.454 --> 00:01:33.576
Yeah, so not only are we getting fossils from a time that we sorely need fossils from,

00:01:34.285 --> 00:01:36.126
we're getting really good fossils.

00:01:36.666 --> 00:01:39.227
And it's been known for over a hundred years.

00:01:39.628 --> 00:01:41.128
So it's got the La Brea thing where...

00:01:41.148 --> 00:01:41.909
It's not new.

00:01:42.049 --> 00:01:44.110
It's not new. We know a lot about it.

00:01:44.310 --> 00:01:47.271
Tons has been collected. Tons has been learned.

00:01:48.012 --> 00:01:53.214
And also, also, it is the site that has given us some of the most famous,

00:01:53.654 --> 00:01:57.556
bizarre animals in the fossil record of all time.

00:01:57.625 --> 00:02:01.207
Yeah, it's an old fossil site, a really good fossil site,

00:02:01.327 --> 00:02:03.428
and everything in it is weird.

00:02:04.008 --> 00:02:09.010
Like, some truly incredible famous creatures, which we will get into,

00:02:09.550 --> 00:02:13.132
will talk about what it is, how it was found, how it formed,

00:02:13.532 --> 00:02:16.734
what's found there, and a bit about why it's so significant.

00:02:17.154 --> 00:02:20.995
Another reason to talk about the Burgess Shale is that it has been requested

00:02:21.485 --> 00:02:27.688
