Will, would you like to start off our news discussion?

00:06:46.172 --> 00:06:48.613
I would, and I would like to talk about baby sharks.

00:06:49.153 --> 00:06:52.775
Now apologies to any parents listening at home right now, I know what I just did.

00:06:53.095 --> 00:06:54.416
He knows, he knows.

00:06:54.616 --> 00:06:55.976
I know, I worked at an aquarium.

00:06:56.923 --> 00:06:59.685
If I had to put up with it, you ought to put up with it.

00:07:00.265 --> 00:07:02.406
We made it through all of episode 48.

00:07:02.646 --> 00:07:02.946
Right.

00:07:02.966 --> 00:07:04.307
Without making a reference to the song.

00:07:05.868 --> 00:07:11.531
This is about a prehistoric, it seems, great white shark nursery.

00:07:11.911 --> 00:07:12.491
Ooh.

00:07:12.912 --> 00:07:17.314
And I'll explain what that is in just a moment, but this is research by Jamie Villafana et al.

00:07:17.644 --> 00:07:22.427
in Scientific Reports, and the article is in Smithsonian Mag by Nora McGreevy.

00:07:22.707 --> 00:07:26.989
Great white sharks, or white sharks as they're sometimes known in different parts of the world.

00:07:27.369 --> 00:07:28.650
For people who think they're not that great.

00:07:28.710 --> 00:07:30.871
Yeah, so-so white sharks.

00:07:31.391 --> 00:07:37.935
Carcharodon carcharias are the famous, biggest predatory shark alive today.

00:07:38.325 --> 00:07:43.928
If you don't know it, then you've never seen anything on TV that deals with sharks.

00:07:44.408 --> 00:07:45.589
It's the shark's shark.

00:07:46.029 --> 00:07:51.732
But something a lot of people don't realize about them is their way of rearing their young,

00:07:51.912 --> 00:07:58.475
if you can call rearing with sharks, but they actually give birth to their young in nursery areas.

00:07:58.926 --> 00:08:02.828
So these typically shallower, typically closer to shore,

00:08:03.248 --> 00:08:07.630
where it's harder for larger predators like themselves to hunt their young,

00:08:08.130 --> 00:08:09.630
is where they typically give birth.

00:08:09.891 --> 00:08:15.793
And I believe that there is suspected to be a nursery area up off the shore of Long Island where I grew up.

00:08:15.973 --> 00:08:18.754
Absolutely. That is one of the few we're aware of.

00:08:19.074 --> 00:08:20.615
We don't understand these well.

00:08:21.035 --> 00:08:23.676
We only know of, the paper only said a few.

00:08:24.003 --> 00:08:27.705
It didn't give many, and there's not many that we can, like, nail down.

00:08:28.025 --> 00:08:30.406
That's one of the most recent ones we've identified.

00:08:30.667 --> 00:08:34.088
Well, if I remember from, I wrote about this as a journalist once or twice.

00:08:34.108 --> 00:08:42.152
I want to remember, we've not actually ever witnessed great whites giving birth and raising young.

00:08:42.912 --> 00:08:44.733
I don't think we've even seen great white mating.

00:08:45.133 --> 00:08:47.775
I don't believe that's ever been witnessed by humans.

00:08:48.204 --> 00:08:54.167
The reason we know these nurseries are in certain areas is because we find an abundance of baby white sharks.

00:08:54.367 --> 00:08:56.568
Right. That's where the youngest sharks are.

00:08:56.748 --> 00:09:00.850
It's when we continuously catch and find baby great whites.

00:09:01.130 --> 00:09:04.292
We're like, all right, this must be close to where they're giving birth.

00:09:04.692 --> 00:09:06.333
So a fossil example?

00:09:06.373 --> 00:09:08.274
This is a fossil example of that.

00:09:08.563 --> 00:09:15.387
Which, as we were just saying, due to the mysteriousness of it today, is really important.

00:09:15.587 --> 00:09:19.509
This is a fossil deposit in the Coquimbo region of northern Chile.

00:09:19.889 --> 00:09:20.949
So, South America.

00:09:21.530 --> 00:09:27.212
And dates to about two and a half to five million years old in the Pliocene.

00:09:27.713 --> 00:09:32.255
And this is one of many sites where they were finding tons of shark teeth.

00:09:32.806 --> 00:09:37.948
And noticed particularly at this site that a lot of the shark teeth are younger.

00:09:38.369 --> 00:09:41.170
And so this was not the only site they were looking at.

00:09:41.210 --> 00:09:45.052
But they started to notice that out of all of them, lots of baby teeth.

00:09:45.312 --> 00:09:48.213
You know, baby shark teeth were coming up from this site.

00:09:48.453 --> 00:09:54.196
Which started to suggest that this may be a fossil example of a great white shark nursery.

00:09:54.605 --> 00:09:58.267
And so that tells them a few important things.

00:09:58.927 --> 00:10:01.868
Now, how did they identify it as baby teeth?

00:10:02.209 --> 00:10:06.510
Is due to the size, is based on the size and shape of the teeth.

00:10:07.131 --> 00:10:11.973
You can differentiate a small shark tooth from a baby shark tooth by certain features.

00:10:12.173 --> 00:10:13.674
I don't know those features, but they do.

00:10:14.474 --> 00:10:15.034
I was wondering.

00:10:15.554 --> 00:10:18.376
And the density of the concentration of teeth.

00:10:18.523 --> 00:10:20.865
Like, it was a very high concentration.

00:10:21.065 --> 00:10:26.107
Suggests that it wasn't a nursery for a short period, but for potentially millions of years.

00:10:26.928 --> 00:10:32.290
That this area was used consistently and babies were dropping teeth there over millennia.

00:10:32.691 --> 00:10:35.432
Gotcha. So it wasn't just that there was a baby shark here.

00:10:35.492 --> 00:10:38.934
There were lots and lots of generations of baby sharks.

00:10:39.074 --> 00:10:43.016
Exactly. So this was a regularly used nursery site.

00:10:43.465 --> 00:10:44.686
If it indeed is a nursery.

00:10:44.966 --> 00:10:48.568
But it was definitely being regularly occupied by small great white sharks.

00:10:48.988 --> 00:10:55.791
This also suggests that great whites were much more common off the Pacific coast of South America.

00:10:55.871 --> 00:10:56.571
Than they are today.

00:10:56.752 --> 00:11:05.136
The density of teeth in this region show that they were occupying that in higher populations than they are today.

00:11:05.156 --> 00:11:07.337
They're fairly rare there today in fact.

00:11:07.745 --> 00:11:11.887
Now they're also not doing well conservation wise in general globally.

00:11:12.207 --> 00:11:12.487
Right.

00:11:12.787 --> 00:11:14.948
So that has to be taken into consideration.

00:11:14.968 --> 00:11:21.490
But still, this is a difference in their distribution than we would expect by today's distribution.

00:11:21.891 --> 00:11:26.792
Something has changed where the best conditions are for having a baby shark.

00:11:26.972 --> 00:11:29.373
Yeah. This is also why they're wanting to look at it.

00:11:29.385 --> 00:11:42.691
Because hopefully understanding a prehistoric nursery will help us better understand what the needs are of the great whites for giving birth and having a nursery.

00:11:43.031 --> 00:11:45.712
How we might best identify nurseries today.

00:11:46.193 --> 00:11:51.555
And therefore protect these sharks because they are apex predators which means really important for the food chain.

00:11:51.825 --> 00:11:55.947
Right. And they're also big which means not doing great in the age of humans.

00:11:55.967 --> 00:12:00.329
Yeah. And they live a long time and they mature slowly.

00:12:00.529 --> 00:12:06.431
I think I'd have to double check the paper but they don't reach sexual maturity until at least a decade.

00:12:07.132 --> 00:12:09.193
Well and also their babies are big.

00:12:09.293 --> 00:12:10.693
Yep. And they only have a few.

00:12:10.993 --> 00:12:16.716
Exactly. I was going to say, once again, I have a vague memory of learning about this when I wrote about it.

00:12:17.065 --> 00:12:23.449
But I want to say the smallest great whites ever found in the ocean are like four feet long.

00:12:23.469 --> 00:12:23.669
Yeah.

00:12:24.169 --> 00:12:26.810
But these are big. They start out big.

00:12:26.951 --> 00:12:27.411
Exactly.

00:12:27.631 --> 00:12:33.634
And you can't have a litter. You're not going to have a pile of four foot babies.

00:12:33.794 --> 00:12:40.658
Exactly. And though great whites are not the elephants of the sea because whales exist, they are facing the same issues that...

00:12:40.667 --> 00:12:41.747
They're the rhinos of the sea.

00:12:41.767 --> 00:12:41.967
Yeah.

00:12:43.748 --> 00:12:51.891
But they are having the same issues and so hopefully we will be able to glean some helpful tidbits from this fossil site.

00:12:52.211 --> 00:12:55.093
A little bit of conservation paleontology.

00:12:55.353 --> 00:12:55.593
Yeah.

00:12:55.613 --> 00:13:02.416
That we discussed in episode eight of the podcast but then also in our recent live chat episode. Go check those out. They're on YouTube and also on your podcast feed.

00:13:03.125 --> 00:13:09.788
My first bit of news is about surprising eating habits of dinosaurs.

00:13:10.289 --> 00:13:10.609
Okay.

00:13:10.989 --> 00:13:18.993
Specifically big predatory dinosaurs. Specifically eating maybe big predatory dinosaurs.

00:13:20.714 --> 00:13:21.674
Oh. Therapod inception.

00:13:23.586 --> 00:13:30.188
This is research in the journal PLOS One by Stephanie Drumheller et al. We know Stephanie.

00:13:30.208 --> 00:13:30.648
Yeah.

00:13:30.689 --> 00:13:37.611
She's just a town over two hours away over in Knoxville. She does crocs and we like her.

00:13:38.672 --> 00:13:41.553
And we'll link to an article in The Guardian by Nicola Davis.

00:13:42.105 --> 00:13:54.729
If you head on over to western Colorado you might find the Migat Mor quarry which is a quarry that digs into a deposit of late Jurassic dinosaur dominated stuff.

00:13:54.789 --> 00:13:57.830
I don't actually have it written down here but Morrison formation I'm pretty sure.

00:13:58.311 --> 00:14:02.472
And there's lots of bite marks on the bones of dinosaurs that are found there.

00:14:02.732 --> 00:14:03.372
Always cool.

00:14:03.884 --> 00:14:17.751
So this research aimed to sort of do an analysis and overview of what are the patterns we see with these bite marks. They examined 2,368 fossil bones.

00:14:17.891 --> 00:14:21.473
It's been around for a while. There's a lot of work on it.

00:14:22.514 --> 00:14:28.317
And they found that 29% of them had bite marks on them.

00:14:28.626 --> 00:14:31.767
Either punctures or scrapes from teeth.

00:14:32.127 --> 00:14:32.728
That's decent.

00:14:32.868 --> 00:14:39.771
That's a lot. Yeah. They point out that that is much higher than we see at other dinosaur dominated sites.

00:14:40.451 --> 00:14:44.813
Even ones from around the same time and age. Or time and place rather.

00:14:45.186 --> 00:14:57.891
The size and shape of these tooth marks. So their large size but also the evidence of serrations on the teeth suggest that a lot of them were being made by large theropod dinosaurs.

00:14:58.091 --> 00:14:59.472
Big carnivorous dinos.

00:14:59.952 --> 00:15:08.075
This quarry has yielded body fossils, so bones, of two different types of large predatory dinosaurs.

00:15:08.545 --> 00:15:12.567
The by far most abundant is Allosaurus. Cool.

00:15:12.687 --> 00:15:16.308
Very famous dinosaur. Also much rarer, Ceratosaurus.

00:15:17.389 --> 00:15:22.611
Yeah. I know Will likes Ceratosaurus. The one with the little horn on his nose.

00:15:22.631 --> 00:15:28.333
So odds are, the researchers posit, that these are Allosaurus bite marks.

00:15:28.353 --> 00:15:31.035
You can narrow it down to a type of animal and a size.

00:15:31.405 --> 00:15:36.667
It's very difficult to say this species made this mark. But...

00:15:37.267 --> 00:15:41.809
We're saying a dinosaur of this size made this bite mark.

00:15:42.169 --> 00:15:45.651
And the dinosaur of this size that's here is Allosaurus.

00:15:46.411 --> 00:15:48.072
You draw from that what you will.

00:15:48.112 --> 00:15:49.332
That's exactly what it is.

00:15:50.673 --> 00:15:55.275
They also noticed patterns on what kinds of bones these are on.

00:15:55.784 --> 00:16:01.867
About 50% of the bites, ish, are on what they called "low economy bones".

00:16:02.387 --> 00:16:04.909
Which is to say, the bones that don't have all the best meat.

00:16:05.229 --> 00:16:10.951
Which suggests that these bites were being made, rather often, by predators who were,

00:16:11.271 --> 00:16:15.693
I believe Stephanie was quoted in the article, saying "late to the party".

00:16:17.346 --> 00:16:22.948
That either all the good stuff was already eaten, or already decomposing.

00:16:23.528 --> 00:16:27.730
So they had to nibble on toes and bones where you don't have quite as much nutrition.

00:16:28.010 --> 00:16:29.731
That's a fantastic description.

00:16:29.791 --> 00:16:33.032
Stephanie is a lot of fun. She's really cool.

00:16:33.492 --> 00:16:38.995
So, the suggestion is that what we're seeing is evidence of fairly common,

00:16:39.245 --> 00:16:41.926
about as common as not, scavenging behavior.

00:16:42.826 --> 00:16:50.089
And then there's the intriguing note that about 70% of the bite marks are on the bones of sauropods.

00:16:50.609 --> 00:16:52.750
So big long-necked herbivorous dinosaurs.

00:16:53.170 --> 00:16:55.812
Which makes total sense. That's a lot of prey.

00:16:56.192 --> 00:16:57.012
It's your bread and butter.

00:16:57.392 --> 00:17:01.154
About 17% are on theropod bones.

00:17:01.744 --> 00:17:06.867
Which means big carnivorous dinos were nomming on big carnivorous dino bodies.

00:17:07.807 --> 00:17:13.770
And, most of the common big theropod bones at this site are allosaurus.

00:17:14.451 --> 00:17:19.633
So if the bite marks are from allosaurus, and the bones are from allosaurus,

