At most fossil sites when we see soft tissue preserved, because we've talked about this before, right, we talked about this with cephalopods in episode 16, we talked about this with pterosaurs in episode 79.

00:50:19.190 --> 00:50:21.391
Boryalopelta, who we mentioned in the news.

00:50:21.947 --> 00:50:34.052
You can get skin and organ type preservation, but a lot of the time when you see soft tissues preserved as fossils, they're mineralized much the same way that bone is.

00:50:34.132 --> 00:50:38.774
Yeah, it's minerals replacing the organic structure.

00:50:39.014 --> 00:50:39.274
Right.

00:50:39.415 --> 00:50:43.296
Though it's not common to happen with soft squishy stuff, it can, just like it happens with bone.

00:50:43.456 --> 00:50:45.237
Absolutely. It's basically petrified.

00:50:45.497 --> 00:50:45.697
Yes.

00:50:46.042 --> 00:50:48.042
More or less as you'd expect bone to be.

00:50:48.322 --> 00:50:50.223
That's not what the Burgess Shale is like.

00:50:50.483 --> 00:50:52.504
Which I also assumed it was.

00:50:52.704 --> 00:50:53.584
Yeah, I did not know this.

00:50:54.024 --> 00:51:02.567
The Burgess Shale preserves components of the original organic material as a carbonaceous film.

00:51:03.185 --> 00:51:18.730
Basically these organisms have been squished between the layers, between the beds of this shale, and compressed into this sort of goo that is organic and has a lot of that original carbon content.

00:51:19.029 --> 00:51:40.816
Partially mineralized, in some cases, by clay minerals or by phosphate, and what you end up with is this dark shadow, sort of, that preserves not only some of the original content, but the shape, the contours of the body, details of organs and tissues and internal structures.

00:51:41.336 --> 00:51:44.117
Sometimes even 3D preserved, although usually flattened.

00:51:44.226 --> 00:51:45.147
Yeah.

00:51:45.207 --> 00:51:51.068
I saw a reference to phosphatized organs maintaining some aspect of three dimensional shape.

00:51:51.468 --> 00:52:00.270
Which is neat. Right now I'm picturing the Looney Tunes running through a window or a wall and leaving their outline.

