00:55:27.248 --> 00:55:32.911
When I did searches for... so I read this great museum site and then I was like, "Alright, I looked for recent research.

00:55:32.931 --> 00:55:35.912
What research about the Burgess Shale has come up in the last several years?"

00:55:36.532 --> 00:55:44.956
And a lot of it is trying to figure out what's special about the Burgess Shale that led to this level of preservation in its fossils.

00:55:45.585 --> 00:55:48.746
A lot of things have been proposed. There are some leading contenders.

00:55:48.966 --> 00:55:51.607
We don't have no idea. We actually have a lot of good ideas.

00:55:52.627 --> 00:55:58.669
It seems to be a combination of rapid preservation and reduced decomposition.

00:55:59.269 --> 00:55:59.709
Okay.

00:56:00.250 --> 00:56:05.611
So certain things are obviously helping things to become preserved.

00:56:05.711 --> 00:56:09.793
Burial was rapid. You have these mudflows that bury things real quick.

00:56:09.905 --> 00:56:18.488
Certain clay minerals, it seems, certain of the minerals in the clay are thought to be particularly good at mineralizing tissues.

00:56:19.089 --> 00:56:29.613
That where the tissues are mineralized, where they are preserved within and by the sediment, it may have to do with what kind of minerals are there?

00:56:29.793 --> 00:56:32.994
The type of clay that was burying these critters.

00:56:33.007 --> 00:56:37.709
Right, right. That those two factors may have helped speed up preservation.

00:56:38.149 --> 00:56:45.112
And then on the other hand, decomposing factors are thought to have been hindered, one, by low oxygen.

00:56:45.312 --> 00:56:45.592
Yeah.

00:56:45.973 --> 00:56:50.334
Deep water, low oxygen. The rapid sealing of the sediment.

00:56:50.843 --> 00:57:00.027
So it's been suggested that the, so, you know, you get sediment deposits, but sediment isn't a solid, it's lots of little bits.

00:57:00.087 --> 00:57:00.348
