01:41:07.004 --> 01:41:17.228
And B, very often used by the scientists to propose them to justify ranking of humans.

01:41:17.308 --> 01:41:20.349
Yeah, discrimination between those differences.

01:41:20.986 --> 01:41:34.652
A lot of the times when you'll see those in the past, there are some very infamous examples of scientists going, "Look, I did some science and I found that black people are not as intelligent as white people with my science."

01:41:34.832 --> 01:41:41.734
There is a whole bunch of those cities aimed at the Tuskegee Airmen and things like that to argue why they shouldn't be pilots.

01:41:42.047 --> 01:41:48.369
And it's, again, not scientifically upheld. That does not work.

01:41:48.789 --> 01:41:52.930
Ben, do you remember at the beginning in our announcement when we talked about racism in sciences?

01:41:53.391 --> 01:42:04.714
This is if you remember the scene from Django Unchained where Leo DiCaprio is talking about these little divots in the skull are what make black people subservient or whatever.

01:42:05.174 --> 01:42:06.715
It's that kind of nonsense.

01:42:07.586 --> 01:42:20.671
Race is far less a biological thing than it is a cultural thing, which does not mean it's not real or significant. Cultural identity is a real...

01:42:21.151 --> 01:42:21.931
It's part of being human.

01:42:21.951 --> 01:42:27.353
Part of being human, it's part of our society, it ties into our politics, it ties into all sorts of things.

01:42:27.904 --> 01:42:34.848
But we can't solidly classify people based on race.

01:42:34.988 --> 01:42:44.512
There is a fundamental difference between culturally identified races and biologically identified species and subspecies.

01:42:44.747 --> 01:42:58.051
That article that I was reading also made the point that if we are comparing, like trying to do that, what we call "black" in terms of a race is more diverse genetically than all other humans.

01:42:58.131 --> 01:42:58.932
Yeah, exactly.

01:43:00.287 --> 01:43:11.311
And there's all this interaction because we're not isolated from each other, we're not living separate lifestyles, we're not living separate lives, we don't have separate habits.

01:43:12.151 --> 01:43:20.054
We're culturally distinct, but species-wise, biologically, we are one giant unit.

01:43:20.327 --> 01:43:33.151
Well, there's the two big issues with it as well as like, if we were to try to find where to draw those lines, there is no clear consistent way to decide where those lines should go.

01:43:33.431 --> 01:43:33.631
Right.

01:43:33.806 --> 01:43:48.111
Because in certain areas, there are multitudes of races that are recognized there and not as recognizable outside of that area, and humans are really good at telling humans apart.

01:43:48.646 --> 01:44:00.591
But I'm sure if we were to ask snakes how they'd categorize themselves, they'd categorize themselves differently than we'd categorize them, but every garter snake looks the same as another garter snake to most of us.

01:44:00.991 --> 01:44:12.095
We notice painfully minute details to differentiate people from people and individuals from individuals, so we're probably also not great sources.

01:44:12.595 --> 01:44:13.535
We're too close to the problem.

01:44:13.545 --> 01:44:14.246
We're too close to it.

01:44:14.266 --> 01:44:24.369
Like, we're too good at telling differences. The fact that we can tell every individual apart 99% of the time, minus like identical twins and really random coincidences.

01:44:24.989 --> 01:44:25.890
And glitches in the matrix.

01:44:25.930 --> 01:44:33.392
Yes, exactly. Is part of the fact we're biologically evolved to tell differences.

01:44:34.006 --> 01:44:38.968
Now, part of the question was, is it scientific or is it a societal taboo?

01:44:39.368 --> 01:44:52.675
And the answer to that last part is it would absolutely be controversial socially to try to split people up like that, and it has been in the past when people have done it.

01:44:53.355 --> 01:44:57.237
Fortunately, we don't have to worry about that because it's not scientific.

01:44:57.428 --> 01:45:00.169
Yeah, it doesn't hold up.

01:45:00.369 --> 01:45:01.209
We don't work that way.

01:45:01.629 --> 01:45:08.112
Thank you for that question, Michael. Good question. There's lots of discussions, I don't want to make it seem like...

01:45:08.412 --> 01:45:10.112
And case closed.

01:45:10.773 --> 01:45:19.696
We two white dudes on the internet did not just fully encapsulate issues of race and divisions among humans.

01:45:19.896 --> 01:45:20.976
Yeah, no, we figured it out.

01:45:20.996 --> 01:45:21.677
We did it. We solved it.

01:45:22.202 --> 01:45:28.404
If you want to learn more, go online. There are some really great articles, and I might put one or two in the blog post.

01:45:28.604 --> 01:45:28.864
Yeah.

01:45:29.424 --> 01:45:33.746
About sort of where science and race kind of meet.

